1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,287
Chapter 33: Complications of the Term Newborn.

3
00:00:06,287 --> 00:00:19,625
Respiratory Distress Neonatal Encephalopathy And Cerebral Palsy Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome Hematological Disorders Newborn Injuries References Term newborns are susceptible to various illnesses and injuries.

4
00:00:19,625 --> 00:00:28,262
In many instances, clinical manifestations of these disorders are extensions of pathological effects already incurred by the fetus.

5
00:00:28,262 --> 00:00:34,575
A common example is the newborn who is depressed and acidotic because of intrapartum septicemia.

6
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:41,950
Susceptibility varies depending upon gestational age, and those more common in term newborns are considered here.

7
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:47,075
Those more frequent in preterm neonates are discussed in Chapter 34.

8
00:00:47,075 --> 00:00:53,787
Specific disorders that are the direct consequence of maternal diseases are discussed in pertinent chapters.

9
00:00:53,787 --> 00:00:56,037
Respiratory Distress.

10
00:00:56,037 --> 00:01:04,587
At the time of delivery, the newborn must convert rapidly to air breathing as described in Chapter 32 (p. 586).

11
00:01:04,587 --> 00:01:11,850
With inspiration, alveoli expand, lung fluid is cleared, and surfactant prevents alveolar collapse.

12
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:16,612
Interference with these functions can create respiratory insufficiency.

13
00:01:16,612 --> 00:01:24,887
The neonate demonstrates hypoxemia and compensatory tachypnea, nasal flaring, chest wall retractions, and grunting.

14
00:01:24,887 --> 00:01:37,412
In preterm neonates, this is often caused by lung immaturity and insufficient surfactant—respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), which is discussed in Chapter 34 (p. 615).

15
00:01:37,412 --> 00:01:43,975
As fetuses approach term, surfactant deficiency as a cause of respiratory distress diminishes.

16
00:01:43,975 --> 00:01:53,725
The leading causes in term newborns are transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN), meconium aspiration syndrome, pneumonia, and pneumothorax.

17
00:01:53,725 --> 00:02:09,550
Less common etiologies are RDS, persistent pulmonary hypertension, acid/base disturbances, central nervous system (CNS) insults, and congenital chest, respiratory tract, CNS, or cardiac anomalies .

18
00:02:09,550 --> 00:02:15,137
Following birth, successful neonatal transition to air breathing may be delayed.

19
00:02:15,137 --> 00:02:21,537
With longer delays, evaluations to identify the cause of respiratory distress are warranted.

20
00:02:21,537 --> 00:02:26,162
The initial neonatal evaluation includes chest radiographs.

21
00:02:26,162 --> 00:02:33,062
Complete blood count or C-reactive protein levels and blood cultures are obtained to identify infection.

22
00:02:33,062 --> 00:02:39,387
Arterial blood gas assessments can define respiratory status and direct oxygenation care.

23
00:02:39,387 --> 00:02:45,887
In general, treatment of respiratory distress is supportive with oxygen therapy provided as needed.

24
00:02:45,887 --> 00:02:54,550
Depending on the etiology and severity, continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) or mechanical ventilation may be needed.

25
00:02:54,550 --> 00:03:01,737
Nutritional intake may be oral, gavage, or intravenous depending on the degree of neonatal tachypnea.

26
00:03:01,737 --> 00:03:07,225
High respiratory rates can limit effective breastfeeding and raise aspiration risk.

27
00:03:07,225 --> 00:03:10,625
# Transient Tachypnea of the Newborn.

28
00:03:10,625 --> 00:03:17,375
TTN is benign, self-limited, and stems from slow clearance of fetal lung fluid after birth.

29
00:03:17,375 --> 00:03:21,062
Neonates of any gestational age can be affected.

30
00:03:21,062 --> 00:03:28,975
However, delivery before 39 weeks’ gestation raises risk, and rates vary inversely with gestational age.

31
00:03:28,975 --> 00:03:38,487
Other risk factors are cesarean delivery without labor, male gender, perinatal asphyxia, and large- or small-for-gestational-age birthweight.

32
00:03:38,487 --> 00:03:42,625
Maternal gestational diabetes or asthma are others.

33
00:03:42,625 --> 00:03:45,862
TTN is a diagnosis of exclusion.

34
00:03:45,862 --> 00:03:51,850
Thus, the evaluation outlined in the last section excludes pathological sources.

35
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:55,600
Treatment is supportive with oxygen therapy as needed.

36
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:59,650
Most TTN cases resolve within 48 hours.

37
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:02,725
# Respiratory Distress Syndrome.

38
00:04:02,725 --> 00:04:08,737
This condition stems from surfactant deficiency and has a relatively low incidence at term .

39
00:04:08,737 --> 00:04:17,475
Risks include male gender, white race, and maternal diabetes (Anadkat, 2012; Li, 2019).

40
00:04:17,475 --> 00:04:24,200
Less frequently, rare mutations in genes that code for surfactant protein synthesis may contribute.

41
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:38,212
Four are surfactant protein B, surfactant protein C, adenosine triphosphate (ATP)-binding cassette subfamily A member 3 (ABCA3), and thyroid transcription factor 1 genes .

42
00:04:38,212 --> 00:04:49,187
Regardless of etiology, when surfactant secretion is diminished, the pulmonary pathophysiology, clinical course, and management are similar to that for preterm infants.

43
00:04:49,187 --> 00:04:54,600
Treatment includes CPAP or mechanical ventilation and potentially surfactant

44
00:04:54,748 --> 00:05:00,448
replacement either through an endotracheal tube or by less invasive surfactant techniques .

45
00:05:00,398 --> 00:05:09,923
Bacterial pneumonia, especially related to group B Streptococcus in a newborn is often clinically and radiographically indistinguishable from RDS.

46
00:05:09,923 --> 00:05:15,323
Thus, empirical antibiotics are often added to respiratory management.

47
00:05:15,323 --> 00:05:22,798
The prognosis in term newborns with RDS largely depends on the cause, severity, and response to treatment.

48
00:05:22,798 --> 00:05:31,498
Antenatal maternal corticosteroid treatment is employed for threatened preterm birth and described in Chapter 45 (p. 802).

49
00:05:31,498 --> 00:05:39,510
In late-preterm fetuses 34 to 37 weeks’ gestation, this therapy also enhances surfactant synthesis .

50
00:05:39,510 --> 00:05:45,998
At Parkland Hospital, corticosteroids are not given in the late-preterm period for this indication.

51
00:05:45,998 --> 00:05:52,460
Neonatal hypoglycemia is a concern with such treatment, and long-term effects are unknown.

52
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:59,198
However, data indicate that hypoglycemia, if promptly treated, creates no adverse sequelae .

53
00:05:59,198 --> 00:06:02,510
# Meconium Aspiration Syndrome.

54
00:06:02,510 --> 00:06:11,023
The physiology of meconium passage and amnionic fluid contamination is considered in Chapter 24 (p. 458).

55
00:06:11,023 --> 00:06:24,348
In some instances, inhalation of meconium-stained fluid at or near delivery causes acute airway obstruction, chemical pneumonitis, surfactant dysfunction or inactivation, and pulmonary hypertension .

56
00:06:24,348 --> 00:06:31,660
If severe, hypoxemia may lead to neonatal death or long-term neurological sequelae in survivors.

57
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:35,023
Meconium-stained fluid in labor is common.

58
00:06:35,023 --> 00:06:41,398
Fortunately, severe aspiration leading to overt respiratory failure is much less frequent.

59
00:06:41,398 --> 00:06:53,160
In one study, the incidence of severe disease was 4 cases per 1000 newborn hospital discharges, and rates rise progressively from 37 to 43 weeks’ gestation .

60
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:56,273
Mortality rates depend on severity.

61
00:06:56,273 --> 00:07:01,273
Fetal morbidity is more often associated with thicker meconium content.

62
00:07:01,273 --> 00:07:10,898
Presumably, in most cases, amnionic fluid is ample to dilute the meconium to permit prompt clearance by normal fetal physiological mechanisms.

63
00:07:10,898 --> 00:07:16,023
Meconium aspiration syndrome still occasionally develops with light staining.

64
00:07:16,023 --> 00:07:21,423
Many newborns are affected after a normal labor and uncomplicated delivery.

65
00:07:21,423 --> 00:07:28,473
However, some associated obstetrical factors include postterm pregnancy and fetal-growth restriction.

66
00:07:28,473 --> 00:07:38,048
These fetuses are at highest risk because diminished amnionic fluid and labor with cord compression or uteroplacental insufficiency are often comorbid.

67
00:07:38,048 --> 00:07:43,273
These can enhance the likelihood of meconium passage that is thick and undiluted .

68
00:07:43,273 --> 00:07:55,898
Prevention Previously, aspiration was thought to be stimulated by fetal hypoxic episodes, and fetal heart rate tracing abnormalities were used to identify fetuses at greatest risk during labor.

69
00:07:55,898 --> 00:08:00,285
Unfortunately, this was found to be an unreliable predictor .

70
00:08:00,285 --> 00:08:06,898
As another potential prevention, oropharyngeal suctioning at the perineum was standard care for a time.

71
00:08:06,898 --> 00:08:21,373
However, this was abandoned when evidence failed to support a reduction in meconium aspiration syndrome incidence or severity (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021b; Vain, 2004).

72
00:08:21,373 --> 00:08:38,135
From other studies, endotracheal intubation and suctioning for both vigorous and nonvigorous meconium-stained neonates provides no benefit and is no longer recommended as a routine (Trevisanuto, 2020; Wiswell, 2000; Wyckoff, 2020).

73
00:08:38,135 --> 00:08:48,573
Because neonates born through meconium may need effective positive pressure ventilation for stabilization, it is still critical that pediatric providers attend such deliveries .

74
00:08:48,573 --> 00:08:59,348
Intrapartum amnioinfusion has been used successfully in laboring women with diminished amnionic fluid volume to help resolve frequent variable fetal heart rate decelerations .

75
00:08:59,348 --> 00:09:05,723
Earlier, it was also studied as a preventive measure in labors complicated by meconium staining.

76
00:09:05,723 --> 00:09:13,123
This practice fails to lower rates of meconium aspiration syndrome in settings with standard peripartum surveillance .

77
00:09:13,123 --> 00:09:21,873
A trial was conducted with almost 2000 women at 36 weeks’ gestation or later and in whom labor was complicated by thick meconium .

78
00:09:21,873 --> 00:09:28,698
The perinatal death rate with and without amnioinfusion was 0.05 percent in both groups.

79
00:09:28,698 --> 00:09:36,285
Rates of moderate or severe meconium aspiration also were not significantly different in those with (4.4

80
00:09:36,436 --> 00:09:41,248
percent) and without (3.1 percent) amnioinfusion.

81
00:09:41,198 --> 00:09:48,098
Last, cesarean delivery rates were similar—32 versus 29 percent, respectively.

82
00:09:48,098 --> 00:09:55,623
Treatment Ventilatory support including CPAP, intubation, and mechanical ventilation is carried out as needed .

83
00:09:55,623 --> 00:10:03,661
Because some aspects of meconium aspiration syndrome are caused by surfactant deficiency, replacement therapy is beneficial .

84
00:10:03,661 --> 00:10:15,536
In a review of randomized trials, authors found that surfactant replacement may reduce the need for extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) but did not lower the mortality rate .

85
00:10:15,536 --> 00:10:30,873
Inhaled corticosteroids may partially ameliorate the severity of meconium aspiration syndrome, but neither inhaled or systemic corticosteroids consistently improve critical outcomes (Garg, 2016; Yeung, 2021).

86
00:10:30,873 --> 00:10:41,586
Newborns with meconium aspiration syndrome and pulmonary hypertension are often treated with inhaled nitric oxide, which reduces rates of death and need for ECMO .

87
00:10:41,586 --> 00:10:49,173
ECMO therapy is reserved for neonates who remain poorly oxygenated despite maximal ventilatory assistance .

88
00:10:49,173 --> 00:10:53,148
The proportion that requires ECMO treatment varies.

89
00:10:53,148 --> 00:11:05,186
From the California Perinatal Quality Care Collaborative, 0.35 percent of more than 5100 cases of meconium aspiration syndrome required ECMO treatment.

90
00:11:05,186 --> 00:11:09,461
The associated mortality rate was 5.3 percent .

91
00:11:09,461 --> 00:11:12,723
Neonatal Encephalopathy And Cerebral.

92
00:11:12,723 --> 00:11:14,261
Palsy.

93
00:11:14,261 --> 00:11:25,948
Few events evoke more apprehension in parents and obstetricians than the specter of “brain injury,” which immediately prompts concerns for disabling cerebral palsy and intellectual disability.

94
00:11:25,948 --> 00:11:33,186
Although most brain disorders or injuries are less profound, history has helped to perpetuate the more dismal outlook.

95
00:11:33,186 --> 00:11:41,898
In his first edition of this textbook, Williams (1903) limited discussions of brain injury to those sustained from birth trauma.

96
00:11:41,898 --> 00:11:51,173
When later editions introduced the concept that asphyxia neonatorum was another cause of cerebral palsy, this too was linked to traumatic birth.

97
00:11:51,173 --> 00:12:03,186
Even as brain damage caused by traumatic delivery became uncommon during the ensuing decades, the belief—albeit erroneous—was that intrapartum events caused most neurological disability.

98
00:12:03,186 --> 00:12:09,411
This was a major reason for the escalating cesarean delivery rate beginning in the 1970s.

99
00:12:09,411 --> 00:12:20,123
Unfortunately, because the genesis of cerebral palsy occurs long before labor in most cases, cesarean delivery did little to mitigate cerebral palsy risks.

100
00:12:20,123 --> 00:12:27,273
Cerebral palsy rates have remained the same for 50 years despite a sixfold rise in cesarean birth rates .

101
00:12:27,273 --> 00:12:37,811
These realizations stimulated scientific investigations to determine the etiopathogenesis of fetal brain disorders, including those leading to cerebral palsy.

102
00:12:37,811 --> 00:12:47,986
Seminal observations, including those of Nelson and Ellenberg (1984, 1985, 1986a), are discussed subsequently.

103
00:12:47,986 --> 00:13:02,398
These investigators are appropriately credited with proving that these neurological disorders are due to complex multifactorial processes caused by a combination of genetic, physiological, environmental, and obstetrical factors.

104
00:13:02,398 --> 00:13:09,348
Importantly, these studies showed that few neurological disorders are associated with peripartum events.

105
00:13:09,348 --> 00:13:16,498
However, continuing international interest was garnered to codify the potential role of intrapartum events.

106
00:13:16,498 --> 00:13:26,736
In 2000, a task force of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists was appointed to study neonatal encephalopathy and cerebral palsy.

107
00:13:26,736 --> 00:13:34,086
The multispecialty coalition reviewed data and provided criteria to define various neonatal brain disorders.

108
00:13:34,086 --> 00:13:43,361
Their findings were promulgated by the American Academy of Pediatrics and American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2003).

109
00:13:43,361 --> 00:13:47,723
Ten years later, a second task force updated the findings .

110
00:13:47,723 --> 00:13:54,061
The 2014 Task Force findings are more circumspect in contrast to the earlier ones.

111
00:13:54,061 --> 00:14:06,498
Specifically, more limitations are cited in identifying cause(s) of peripartum hypoxicischemic encephalopathy (HIE) compared with other etiologies of neonatal encephalopathy.

112
00:14:06,498 --> 00:14:12,773
The 2014 Task Force recommends multidimensional assessment of each affected infant.

113
00:14:12,773 --> 00:14:20,898
They add the caveat that no one strategy is infallible, and thus no single strategy will achieve 100-percent certainty in

114
00:14:21,052 --> 00:14:24,852
attributing a cause to neonatal encephalopathy.

115
00:14:24,802 --> 00:14:27,964
# Neonatal Encephalopathy.

116
00:14:27,964 --> 00:14:41,214
The 2014 Task Force defined neonatal encephalopathy as a syndrome of neurological dysfunction identified in the earliest days of life in neonates born at ≥35 weeks’ gestation.

117
00:14:41,214 --> 00:14:52,352
It is manifested by seizures or subnormal levels of consciousness and is often accompanied by difficulty with initiating and maintaining respiration and by depressed tone and reflexes.

118
00:14:52,352 --> 00:15:10,377
The incidence of encephalopathy has been cited to be 1 to 2 cases per 1000 term liveborn neonates, and it is much more frequent in preterm newborns (Ensing, 2015; Laptook, 2016; Novak, 2018; Ravichandran, 2020).

119
00:15:10,377 --> 00:15:23,102
The 2014 Task Force concluded that many etiologies may lead to encephalopathy and cerebral palsy, however, it focused on HIE and causes that were thought to be incurred intrapartum.

120
00:15:23,102 --> 00:15:35,639
To identify affected infants, a thorough evaluation is necessary and includes maternal history, obstetrical antecedents, intrapartum factors, placental pathology, and newborn course.

121
00:15:35,639 --> 00:15:40,089
These are complemented by laboratory and neuroimaging findings.

122
00:15:40,089 --> 00:15:44,427
Three clinically defined levels categorize encephalopathy.

123
00:15:44,427 --> 00:15:52,739
Mild encephalopathy is characterized by hyperalertness, irritability, jitteriness, and hypertonia and hypotonia.

124
00:15:52,739 --> 00:15:58,627
Moderate encephalopathy shows lethargy, abnormal tone, and occasional seizures.

125
00:15:58,627 --> 00:16:04,939
Severe encephalopathy is manifest by coma, multiple seizures, and recurrent apnea.

126
00:16:04,939 --> 00:16:15,802
The 2014 Task Force also concluded that of the several forms of cerebral palsy, only the spastic quadriplegic type can result from acute peripartum ischemia.

127
00:16:15,802 --> 00:16:25,577
Other forms—hemiparetic or hemiplegic cerebral palsy, spastic diplegia, and ataxia—are unlikely to result from an intrapartum event.

128
00:16:25,577 --> 00:16:34,164
Purely dyskinetic or ataxic cerebral palsy, especially when accompanied by a learning disorder, usually has a genetic origin .

129
00:16:34,164 --> 00:16:48,764
Criteria for Hypoxic-Ischemic Encephalopathy The 2014 Task Force radically revised the 2003 criteria used to define an acute peripartum event that is consistent with HIE and neonatal encephalopathy.

130
00:16:48,764 --> 00:16:54,564
These are outlined in Table 33-1 and are considered with the following caveats .

131
00:16:54,564 --> 00:16:57,089
Table 33-1.

132
00:16:57,089 --> 00:17:10,639
Findings Consistent with an Acute Peripartum or Intrapartum Event Leading to Hypoxic-Ischemic Encephalopathy First, Apgar scores that are low at 5 and 10 minutes are associated with greater risk for neurological impairment.

133
00:17:10,639 --> 00:17:17,139
Low scores stem from many causes, and most of these infants will not develop cerebral palsy.

134
00:17:17,139 --> 00:17:24,964
With a 5-minute Apgar ≥7, it is unlikely that peripartum HIE caused cerebral palsy.

135
00:17:24,964 --> 00:17:29,627
Acid-base study results define a second HIE criterion.

136
00:17:29,627 --> 00:17:37,027
Low pH and high base deficit levels raise the likelihood that neonatal encephalopathy was caused by HIE.

137
00:17:37,027 --> 00:17:44,877
Decreasing pH levels form a continuum of increasing risk, but most acidemic neonates will be neurologically normal .

138
00:17:44,877 --> 00:17:52,414
A cord artery pH ≥7.2 is very unlikely to be associated with HIE.

139
00:17:52,414 --> 00:18:02,452
Magnetic resonance (MR) imaging or MR spectroscopy (MRS) is the best modality with which to visualize findings consistent with HIE.

140
00:18:02,452 --> 00:18:11,802
The 2014 Task Force concluded that cranial sonography and computed tomography (CT) lack sensitivity in the term newborn.

141
00:18:11,802 --> 00:18:20,889
Normal imaging findings after the first 24 hours of life, however, effectively exclude a hypoxic-ischemic cause of encephalopathy.

142
00:18:20,889 --> 00:18:35,789
MR imaging between 24 and 96 hours may be more sensitive for the timing of peripartum cerebral injury, and MR imaging at 7 to 21 days following birth is the best technique to delineate the full extent of cerebral injury.

143
00:18:35,789 --> 00:18:41,039
Last, multisystem involvement of injury is consistent with HIE.

144
00:18:41,039 --> 00:18:50,202
These include renal, gastrointestinal, hepatic, or cardiac injury; hematological abnormalities; or combinations of these.

145
00:18:50,202 --> 00:18:56,764
The severity of neurological injury does not necessarily correlate with injuries to these other systems.

146
00:18:56,764 --> 00:19:04,702
The 2014 Task Force also found that certain contributing factors may be consistent with an acute peripartum event.

147
00:19:04,702 --> 00:19:12,139
Of these, sentinel events are considered adverse obstetrical events that may lead to catastrophic clinical outcomes.

148
00:19:12,139 --> 00:19:18,489
Examples include ruptured uterus, severe placental abruption, cord prolapse, and

149
00:19:18,652 --> 00:19:21,277
amnionic fluid embolism.

150
00:19:21,227 --> 00:19:31,952
Martinez-Biarge and associates (2012) studied almost 58,000 deliveries and identified 192 cases with one of these sentinel events.

151
00:19:31,952 --> 00:19:42,752
Of these 192 fetus/newborns, 6 percent died intrapartum or in the early neonatal period, and 10 percent developed neonatal encephalopathy.

152
00:19:42,752 --> 00:20:04,152
Other risk factors for neonatal acidosis include prior or emergent cesarean delivery, maternal age ≥35 years, maternal obesity, thick meconium, chorioamnionitis, and general anesthesia (Johansson, 2018; Johnson, 2014; Nelson, 2014).

153
00:20:04,152 --> 00:20:15,664
Differentiating an abnormal fetal heart rate (FHR) tracing on presentation versus one that develops subsequently also was emphasized by the 2014 Task Force.

154
00:20:15,664 --> 00:20:29,827
A category 1 or 2 FHR tracing associated with Apgar scores ≥7 at 5 minutes, normal cord gases (±1 SD), or both are not consistent with an acute HIE event .

155
00:20:29,827 --> 00:20:45,564
An FHR pattern at the time of presentation with persistently minimal or absent variability and lacking accelerations, with duration ≥60 minutes, and even without decelerations is suggestive of an already compromised fetus .

156
00:20:45,564 --> 00:20:57,089
The 2014 Task Force further recommended that with these findings present, if fetal well-being cannot be established, the woman should be evaluated for the method and timing of delivery.

157
00:20:57,089 --> 00:21:05,464
Prevention In term newborns, one of the best-studied interventions for neonatal HIE is postnatally induced hypothermia.

158
00:21:05,464 --> 00:21:22,302
Cooling initiated within the first 6 hours of life to a level of 33.5°C for 72 hours reduces death and moderate to severe neurological disability in term newborns (Jacobs, 2013; Natarajan, 2016b).

159
00:21:22,302 --> 00:21:32,527
One metaanalysis of more than 1500 neonates concluded that hypothermia improves survival rates and neurodevelopment by 24 months of age .

160
00:21:32,527 --> 00:21:41,889
This neuroprotection extends to childhood (Azzopardi, 2014; Guillet, 2012; Shankaran, 2012).

161
00:21:41,889 --> 00:21:53,752
MR imaging studies have demonstrated a slowing of diffusional abnormalities and fewer infarctions with hypothermia (Bednarek, 2012; Natarajan, 2016b).

162
00:21:53,752 --> 00:21:59,489
Longer or deeper cooling does not offer additional protection compared with standard cooling .

163
00:21:59,489 --> 00:22:09,127
Importantly, hospitals should develop plans for timely transfer of encephalopathic newborns to a cooling center for evaluation and possible treatment.

164
00:22:09,127 --> 00:22:15,877
A trial of “late” hypothermia, with cooling initiated after 6 hours of life, showed some benefit.

165
00:22:15,877 --> 00:22:22,277
However, brain protection was not as robust as with cooling initiated in the first 6 hours .

166
00:22:22,277 --> 00:22:28,977
Since therapeutic hypothermia is not completely protective, adjuvant therapies are being explored.

167
00:22:28,977 --> 00:22:35,564
In preclinical HIE models, erythropoietin has both early and late beneficial effects.

168
00:22:35,564 --> 00:22:44,902
These include antiapoptosis and antiinflammatory effects and greater neurogenesis, oligodendrogenesis, and angiogenesis .

169
00:22:44,902 --> 00:22:55,477
One metaanalysis of small clinical trials evaluating this therapy for neuroprophylaxis reports some reductions in cognitive impairment and cerebral palsy rates .

170
00:22:55,477 --> 00:23:00,514
A large national trial has completed enrollment and is in the follow-up phase .

171
00:23:00,514 --> 00:23:08,127
Other adjuvant therapies under investigation are allopurinol, xenon, melatonin, and stem cell therapies .

172
00:23:08,127 --> 00:23:10,739
# Cerebral Palsy.

173
00:23:10,739 --> 00:23:20,077
This term refers to a group of nonprogressive disorders of movement or posture caused by abnormal development or damage to brain centers for motor control.

174
00:23:20,077 --> 00:23:34,889
Cerebral palsy is further classified by the type of neurological dysfunction—spastic, dyskinetic, or ataxic—and by the number and distribution of limbs involved—quadriplegia, diplegia, hemiplegia, or monoplegia.

175
00:23:34,889 --> 00:23:52,327
Together, the major types are spastic quadriplegia—the most common—which has a strong association with mental retardation and seizure disorders; diplegia, which is common in preterm or low-birthweight neonates; hemiplegia; choreoathetoid types; and mixed varieties.

176
00:23:52,327 --> 00:24:04,152
Although epilepsy and intellectual disability frequently accompany cerebral palsy, these two disorders seldom are associated with perinatal asphyxia in the absence of cerebral palsy.

177
00:24:04,152 --> 00:24:17,314
Incidence and Epidemiological Correlates According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2021), the prevalence of cerebral palsy in the United States approximates 3 cases in 1000

178
00:24:17,476 --> 00:24:19,038
children.

179
00:24:18,988 --> 00:24:25,651
It is crucial to emphasize that this rate is derived from all children—including those born preterm.

180
00:24:25,651 --> 00:24:37,588
Because of the remarkably greater survival rates of the latter currently, and despite the elevated cesarean delivery rate, the overall rate of cerebral palsy has remained essentially unchanged .

181
00:24:37,588 --> 00:24:52,513
For example, neonates born at 23 to 27 weeks’ gestation in a Swedish birth cohort had a cerebral palsy incidence of 105 cases per 1000 infants alive at 1 year (Hafström, 2018).

182
00:24:52,513 --> 00:24:56,513
Similar findings have been reported for Australian births .

183
00:24:56,513 --> 00:25:05,213
In absolute numbers, term newborns constitute half of cerebral palsy cases because preterm births are proportionately far fewer.

184
00:25:05,213 --> 00:25:13,213
It is again emphasized that most studies of cerebral palsy rates have not made distinctions between term and preterm neonates.

185
00:25:13,213 --> 00:25:15,763
Figure 33-1.

186
00:25:15,763 --> 00:25:21,338
Elective and emergency cesarean deliveries and live births with cerebral palsy.

187
00:25:21,338 --> 00:25:37,538
Reproduced with permission from Nelson KB, Blair E: Prenatal factors in singletons with cerebral palsy born at or near term, N Engl J Med. 2015 Sep 3;373(10):946–953.

188
00:25:37,538 --> 00:25:48,788
As noted earlier, Nelson and Ellenberg (1984, 1985, 1986a) made many fundamental observations concerning cerebral palsy.

189
00:25:48,788 --> 00:25:54,038
Their initial studies emanated from data from the Collaborative Perinatal Project.

190
00:25:54,038 --> 00:26:00,101
This included children from almost 54,000 pregnancies who were followed until age 7.

191
00:26:00,101 --> 00:26:20,026
They found that the most frequently associated risk factors for cerebral palsy were: (1) evidence of genetic abnormalities such as maternal mental retardation or fetal congenital malformations; (2) birthweight <2000 g; (3) birth before 32 weeks; and (4) perinatal infection.

192
00:26:20,026 --> 00:26:29,488
They also found that obstetrical complications were not strongly predictive, and only a fifth of affected children had markers of perinatal asphyxia.

193
00:26:29,488 --> 00:26:40,926
For the first time, solid evidence supported that the cause of most cases of cerebral palsy was unknown, and importantly, only a small proportion stemmed from neonatal HIE.

194
00:26:40,926 --> 00:26:47,838
Equally importantly, no foreseeable single intervention would likely prevent a large proportion of cases.

195
00:26:47,838 --> 00:26:55,176
Numerous studies have since confirmed many of these findings and have identified an imposing list of other risk factors .

196
00:26:55,176 --> 00:27:00,651
As expected, preterm birth continues to be the single most important risk .

197
00:27:00,651 --> 00:27:04,863
Small-for-gestational-age neonates also are at higher risk.

198
00:27:04,863 --> 00:27:12,576
In one study, cerebral palsy was due to antepartum factors in more than 90 percent of growth-restricted newborns .

199
00:27:12,576 --> 00:27:15,126
Table 33-2.

200
00:27:15,126 --> 00:27:26,276
Perinatal Risk Factors Reported to Be Increased in Children with Cerebral Palsy Many other placental and neonatal risk factors are associated with neurodevelopmental abnormalities .

201
00:27:26,276 --> 00:27:31,751
Some placental factors are discussed further in Chapter 6 (p. 111).

202
00:27:31,751 --> 00:27:40,663
One example is the substantively greater risk from chorioamnionitis (Shevell, 2014; Shi, 2017).

203
00:27:40,663 --> 00:27:52,713
Of neonatal causes, one is arterial ischemic stroke, which may be associated with inherited fetal thrombophilias (Harteman, 2013; Wanenaar, 2018).

204
00:27:52,713 --> 00:28:02,313
Additionally, newborns with isolated congenital heart lesions have an elevated risk for microcephaly, possibly due to chronic fetal hypoxemia .

205
00:28:02,313 --> 00:28:23,263
Other miscellaneous etiologies of cerebral palsy include fetal anemia, twin-twin transfusion syndrome, intrauterine transfusions, and fetal alcohol syndrome (DeJong, 2012; Nelson, 2015; O’Leary, 2012; Rossi, 2011; Schou, 2019).

206
00:28:23,263 --> 00:28:32,801
Apart from these causes, intrapartum hypoxemia was linked to only a few cases of cerebral palsy by the National Collaborative Perinatal Project.

207
00:28:32,801 --> 00:28:38,676
The contribution of HIE to subsequent neurological disorders was discussed earlier.

208
00:28:38,676 --> 00:28:52,426
The 2003 Task Force applied these criteria to more contemporaneous outcomes and determined that only 1.6 cases of cerebral palsy per 10,000 deliveries are attributable solely to intrapartum hypoxia.

209
00:28:52,426 --> 00:29:09,288
This finding is supported by a study of nearly 1000 infants in which fetal-growth restriction and birth defects recognized by age 6 years were more substantial contributors to cerebral palsy and neonatal death than potentially asphyxial birth events and inflammation .

210
00:29:09,288 --> 00:29:14,626
Intrapartum Fetal Heart Rate Monitoring Despite persistent attempts to validate

211
00:29:14,788 --> 00:29:26,650
continuous intrapartum electronic FHR monitoring as effective to prevent adverse perinatal outcomes, evidence does not support its ability to predict or reduce cerebral palsy risk .

212
00:29:26,600 --> 00:29:38,588
Moreover, no specific FHR patterns is predictive of cerebral palsy, and no relationship has been found between the clinician’s response to abnormal patterns and neurological outcome.

213
00:29:38,588 --> 00:29:50,200
Efforts using computer-assisted analysis of FHR tracings have not enhanced predictions (Alfirevic, 2017; INFANT Collaborative Group, 2017).

214
00:29:50,200 --> 00:29:59,675
Indeed, an abnormal heart rate pattern in fetuses that ultimately develop cerebral palsy may reflect a preexisting neurological abnormality .

215
00:29:59,675 --> 00:30:14,375
Because of these studies, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a; 2021a) concludes that electronic FHR monitoring does not reduce the incidence of long-term neurological impairment.

216
00:30:14,375 --> 00:30:22,225
Apgar Scores In general, 1- and 5-minute Apgar scores are poor predictors of long-term neurological impairment .

217
00:30:22,225 --> 00:30:35,050
However, if the 5-minute Apgar score is ≤3, neonatal death or the risk of neurological sequelae rises substantially (Chen, 2020; Nelson, 1984).

218
00:30:35,050 --> 00:30:41,013
In a Swedish study, 5 percent of such children subsequently required special schooling .

219
00:30:41,013 --> 00:30:52,663
In a Scottish study, the incidence of these low Apgar scores was 0.3 percent in more than 750,000 children who were born at term and were in national school census records.

220
00:30:52,663 --> 00:31:03,150
Low Apgar score was strongly associated in a dose-dependent manner with the need for additional educational support in later life, after adjusting for confounding factors.

221
00:31:03,150 --> 00:31:11,688
The strongest association was for the support of physical and motor impairments and was followed by visual, hearing, and cognitive impairments .

222
00:31:11,688 --> 00:31:20,163
Persistence past 5 minutes of these extremely low scores correlates strongly with a higher risk for neurological morbidity and death .

223
00:31:20,163 --> 00:31:30,450
This of course is not absolute, and the 2003 Task Force cited a 10percent risk for cerebral palsy for neonates with 10-minute scores of 0 to 3.

224
00:31:30,450 --> 00:31:40,000
For 15-minute scores ≤2, the cited mortality rate was 53 percent, and cerebral palsy rate was 36 percent.

225
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:48,888
For 20-minute scores ≤2, the mortality rate was 60 percent, and cerebral palsy rate was 57 percent.

226
00:31:48,888 --> 00:31:59,100
Outcomes from a Norwegian population study of neonates who were born from low-risk pregnancies and who had low 5-minute Apgar scores are shown in Table 333 .

227
00:31:59,100 --> 00:32:01,688
Table 33-3.

228
00:32:01,688 --> 00:32:28,413
Comparison of Mortality and Morbidity in Norwegian Infants Weighing >2500 g According to 5-Minute Apgar Scores Umbilical Cord Blood Gas Studies Objective evidence of metabolic acidosis, that is, cord arterial blood pH <7.0 and base deficit ≥12 mmol/L, is a risk factor for encephalopathy and for cerebral palsy (p. 601).

229
00:32:28,413 --> 00:32:31,738
The risk accrues as acidosis worsens.

230
00:32:31,738 --> 00:32:42,288
From one review of 51 studies, low pH in arterial umbilical cord blood correlates with greater risk for neonatal encephalopathy and cerebral palsy .

231
00:32:42,288 --> 00:32:49,675
When used alone, however, these determinations are not accurate in predicting long-term neurological sequelae .

232
00:32:49,675 --> 00:33:02,025
Data from several studies corroborate that a pH <7.0 is the threshold for clinically significant acidemia (Gilstrap, 1989; Goldaber, 1991).

233
00:33:02,025 --> 00:33:09,338
The likelihood of neonatal death grows as the cord artery pH falls to ≤7.0.

234
00:33:09,338 --> 00:33:20,125
Casey and colleagues (2001) reported that when the pH was ≤6.8, the neonatal mortality rate rose 1400-fold.

235
00:33:20,125 --> 00:33:30,963
When the cord pH was ≤7.0 and the 5-minute Apgar score was 0 to 3, the risk of neonatal death rose 3200-fold.

236
00:33:30,963 --> 00:33:42,913
In a study from Oxford, the rate of adverse neurological outcomes was 0.36 percent with pH <7.1 and was 3 percent with pH <7.0 .

237
00:33:42,913 --> 00:33:51,125
A systematic review that includes 12 studies suggests that cord blood lactate levels also help predict neonatal outcomes .

238
00:33:51,125 --> 00:34:01,263
Nucleated Red Blood Cells and Lymphocytes Both immature red cells and lymphocytes enter the circulation of term newborns in response to hypoxia or hemorrhage.

239
00:34:01,263 --> 00:34:17,588
During the past two decades, quantification of these cells has been proposed as a measure of hypoxia, but not all studies support this premise (Boskabadi, 2017; Silva, 2006; Walsh, 2011, 2013).

240
00:34:17,588 --> 00:34:22,200
# Neuroimaging Studies in Encephalopathy and Cerebral.

241
00:34:22,200 --> 00:34:23,738
Palsy

242
00:34:23,908 --> 00:34:33,508
Various neuroimaging techniques have provided important insight into the etiology and evolution of perinatal HIE and later cerebral palsy.

243
00:34:33,458 --> 00:34:42,095
Findings vary by fetal age, and the preterm neonatal brain responds differently to an ischemic episode than that of a term newborn.

244
00:34:42,095 --> 00:34:49,358
Other factors include insult severity and duration and restoration of cerebrovascular hypoperfusion.

245
00:34:49,358 --> 00:34:55,370
Thus, precise timing of an injury with neuroimaging studies is not a realistic goal.

246
00:34:55,370 --> 00:35:04,608
Moreover, the grade of neonatal encephalopathy, that is, mild, moderate, or severe, does not correlate with MR imaging findings .

247
00:35:04,608 --> 00:35:17,333
Neuroimaging in the Neonatal Period The 2014 Task Force concluded that for term newborns, imaging studies are helpful in timing an injury, but they provide only a time window that is imprecise.

248
00:35:17,333 --> 00:35:28,745
The 2014 Task Force reported that these head imaging techniques provide the following information: 1. Neonatal sonography findings are generally normal on the day of birth.

249
00:35:28,745 --> 00:35:36,608
With injury, the thalami and basal ganglia become increasingly echogenic beginning at approximately 24 hours.

250
00:35:36,608 --> 00:35:41,770
This progresses over 2 to 3 days and persists for 5 to 7 days.

251
00:35:41,770 --> 00:35:46,908
2. CT images are usually normal the first day in term newborns.

252
00:35:46,908 --> 00:35:56,783
With injury, the thalami or basal ganglia show decreased density beginning at approximately 24 hours, and this persists for 5 to 7 days.

253
00:35:56,783 --> 00:36:01,783
3. MR imaging will detect some abnormalities on the first day.

254
00:36:01,783 --> 00:36:10,783
Within 24 hours, MR imaging may show restricted water diffusion that peaks at approximately 5 days and disappears within 2 weeks.

255
00:36:10,783 --> 00:36:19,708
T1- and T2- weighted images display variable abnormalities, which have an onset from less than 24 hours to several days.

256
00:36:19,708 --> 00:36:31,458
In a study of 175 term neonates with acute encephalopathy, MR imaging that showed basal ganglia lesions accurately predicted motor impairment at 2 years of age .

257
00:36:31,458 --> 00:36:41,283
Neuroimaging in Older Children with Cerebral Palsy Imaging studies performed in children diagnosed with cerebral palsy frequently show abnormal findings.

258
00:36:41,283 --> 00:36:53,808
Wu and associates (2006) used CT or MR imaging to study 273 children who were born after 36 weeks’ gestation and who were later diagnosed with cerebral palsy.

259
00:36:53,808 --> 00:37:06,670
Although a third of these imaging studies were normal, focal arterial infarction was seen in 22 percent; brain malformations in 14 percent; and periventricular white-matter injuries in 12 percent.

260
00:37:06,670 --> 00:37:17,708
In another study of 351 children with cerebral palsy, of whom approximately half were born near term, MR imaging findings were abnormal in 88 percent .

261
00:37:17,708 --> 00:37:25,033
One MR imaging study found pathological findings in 84 percent of children with spastic quadriplegia .

262
00:37:25,033 --> 00:37:33,283
Remember, the 2014 Task Force concluded that this neurological lesion correlated with neonatal encephalopathy.

263
00:37:33,283 --> 00:37:39,933
Imaging techniques in older children also help define the timing of fetal or perinatal cerebral injury.

264
00:37:39,933 --> 00:37:46,983
Due to concerns regarding the radiation exposure of CT scans, MR imaging is more often used.

265
00:37:46,983 --> 00:37:58,308
Wiklund and coworkers (1991a,b) studied 83 children between ages 5 and 16 years who were born at term and who developed hemiplegic cerebral palsy.

266
00:37:58,308 --> 00:38:08,608
Nearly 75 percent had abnormal CT findings, and these investigators concluded that more than half had CT changes that suggested a prenatal injury.

267
00:38:08,608 --> 00:38:13,270
Approximately 20 percent were attributed to a perinatal injury.

268
00:38:13,270 --> 00:38:17,295
# Intellectual Disability and Seizure Disorders.

269
00:38:17,295 --> 00:38:20,995
These both frequently accompany cerebral palsy.

270
00:38:20,995 --> 00:38:31,583
However, when either manifests alone, it is seldom caused by perinatal hypoxia (Nelson, 1984, 1986a, 2015).

271
00:38:31,583 --> 00:38:43,270
Severe mental disability has a prevalence of 3 cases per 1000 children, and its most frequent causes are chromosomal, gene mutation, and other congenital malformations.

272
00:38:43,270 --> 00:38:47,420
Last, preterm birth also is associated with these .

273
00:38:47,420 --> 00:39:00,995
The major predictors of seizure disorders are fetal malformations— cerebral and noncerebral; family history of seizures; and neonatal seizures (Nelson, 1986b; Pisani, 2015).

274
00:39:00,995 --> 00:39:06,108
Neonatal encephalopathy causes a small proportion of seizure disorders.

275
00:39:06,108 --> 00:39:18,483
Reports from the Neonatal Research Network and other studies conclude that increasing severity of encephalopathy correlates best with seizures (Glass, 2011; Kwon, 2011).

276
00:39:18,483 --> 00:39:20,120
Autism

277
00:39:20,284 --> 00:39:30,884
Spectrum Disorders According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the frequency of autism spectrum disorders is 15 cases per 1000 8-year-old children .

278
00:39:30,834 --> 00:39:38,484
Although these may be associated with maternal metabolic conditions, none has been linked convincingly to peripartum events .

279
00:39:38,484 --> 00:39:41,184
Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome.

280
00:39:41,184 --> 00:39:47,621
This is a drug-withdrawal syndrome that most commonly follows in-utero exposure to maternal opioids.

281
00:39:47,621 --> 00:39:52,609
It also can complicate exposure to alcohol or benzodiazepines.

282
00:39:52,609 --> 00:40:00,159
The syndrome shows hypertonia, autonomic instability, irritability, poor sucking reflex, and seizures .

283
00:40:00,159 --> 00:40:07,346
Its incidence has risen six- to sevenfold during the past decade, coincidental with growing opioid use.

284
00:40:07,346 --> 00:40:16,284
For example, 4 percent of all neonatal intensive care unit days in 2013 were attributed to care of these affected newborns .

285
00:40:16,284 --> 00:40:21,446
Care includes close observation, and pharmacotherapy is usually given.

286
00:40:21,446 --> 00:40:29,221
In addition to morphine and methadone, other treatment may include phenobarbital, benzodiazepines, and clonidine .

287
00:40:29,221 --> 00:40:40,884
More recently, buprenorphine compared with morphine was reported to result in shorter lengths of treatment and hospital stays (Kraft, 2017; Lee, 2019).

288
00:40:40,884 --> 00:40:44,896
Consensus is lacking regarding the most effective regimen.

289
00:40:44,896 --> 00:40:59,046
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the American Society of Addiction Medicine (2019c) have taken the lead in screening, intervention, and treatment of opioid use disorders in pregnant women .

290
00:40:59,046 --> 00:41:01,546
Hematological Disorders.

291
00:41:01,546 --> 00:41:08,896
Obstetricians should be familiar with a few neonatal disorders of erythrocytes, platelets, and coagulation.

292
00:41:08,896 --> 00:41:13,196
Many of these manifest in the fetus and persist in the newborn.

293
00:41:13,196 --> 00:41:15,309
# Anemia.

294
00:41:15,309 --> 00:41:27,534
After 35 weeks’ gestation, the mean cord hemoglobin concentration approximates 17 g/dL, and values below 14 g/dL are considered abnormal.

295
00:41:27,534 --> 00:41:36,034
In newborns, delayed umbilical cord clamping raises hemoglobin levels at birth and improves iron stores in the first several months.

296
00:41:36,034 --> 00:41:48,184
A review of nearly 9200 late-preterm and term deliveries found delayed cord clamping was associated with a mean neonatal hemoglobin rise of 1.2 g/dL at birth .

297
00:41:48,184 --> 00:41:55,234
However, rates of infant anemia at 6 months were similar in groups with or without delayed cord clamping.

298
00:41:55,234 --> 00:42:03,646
Moreover, this practice raises the incidence of hyperbilirubinemia that requires phototherapy but does not prolong hospital stay.

299
00:42:03,646 --> 00:42:17,559
Discussed further in Chapter 27 (p. 500), the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020) recommends a 30- to 60-second delay in cord clamping in all healthy newborns.

300
00:42:17,559 --> 00:42:22,034
Significant neonatal anemia may predate or follow delivery.

301
00:42:22,034 --> 00:42:27,834
First, neonatal anemia may reflect an extension of antenatal fetal anemia.

302
00:42:27,834 --> 00:42:43,971
General etiological groups are immune-medicated red cell destruction, aneuploidy, hematology-related genetic defects, fetomaternal hemorrhage, fetal infection, and placental abnormalities such as chorioangioma or twin anastomotic defects.

303
00:42:43,971 --> 00:42:52,796
Second, at delivery, acute anemia with hypovolemia can follow laceration of the placenta, fetal vessel, or umbilical cord.

304
00:42:52,796 --> 00:43:01,009
Intracranial or extracranial injury or trauma to fetal intraabdominal organs also can cause hemorrhage with acute anemia .

305
00:43:01,009 --> 00:43:03,596
# Polycythemia.

306
00:43:03,596 --> 00:43:19,209
Neonatal polycythemia can be associated with chronic or acute fetal hypoxia, twin anemia-polycythemia syndrome, maternal diabetes or hypertension, placental- and fetal-growth restriction, aneuploidy, and delayed cord clamping.

307
00:43:19,209 --> 00:43:25,146
When the hematocrit rises above 65, blood viscosity markedly increases.

308
00:43:25,146 --> 00:43:30,184
Routine neonatal screening is not recommended in newborns without symptoms .

309
00:43:30,184 --> 00:43:39,396
Frequent signs are hypotonia, a ruddy complexion (plethora), respiratory distress with cyanosis, jitteriness, or hypoglycemia.

310
00:43:39,396 --> 00:43:46,634
Because of the shorter life span of macrocytic fetal erythrocytes, hyperbilirubinemia commonly follows.

311
00:43:46,634 --> 00:43:52,809
Associated blood hyperviscosity can lead to end-organ damage and neurodevelopmental harm.

312
00:43:52,809 --> 00:44:04,484
Isovolemic partial exchange transfusion (PET) with saline can lower the hematocrit and improve symptoms, however, it is not neuroprotective and has associated complications .

313
00:44:04,484 --> 00:44:10,946
From limited data, PET is often reserved for neonates with acute symptoms or hypoglycemia .

314
00:44:10,946 --> 00:44:12,646
#

315
00:44:12,796 --> 00:44:15,083
Hyperbilirubinemia.

316
00:44:15,033 --> 00:44:19,796
Excessive serum bilirubin levels can be neurotoxic for newborns.

317
00:44:19,796 --> 00:44:30,208
In fetuses, hepatic maturation is incomplete, and some unconjugated bilirubin is cleared by placental transfer to be conjugated and excreted by the mother.

318
00:44:30,208 --> 00:44:39,233
After delivery, clearance relies on neonatal hepatic function, and thus varying degrees of neonatal hyperbilirubinemia arise.

319
00:44:39,233 --> 00:44:47,708
Even in the mature newborn, serum bilirubin levels usually increase for 3 to 4 days to reach up to 10 mg/dL.

320
00:44:47,708 --> 00:44:51,833
After this, concentrations usually fall rapidly.

321
00:44:51,833 --> 00:44:57,746
In approximately 15 percent of term newborns, bilirubin levels cause jaundice.

322
00:44:57,746 --> 00:45:11,808
Of risks, polycythemia, hemolysis such as from ABO incompatibility or glucose-6phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, and extravasation of blood such as from a cephalohematoma all raise bilirubin burden.

323
00:45:11,808 --> 00:45:15,808
Decreased bilirubin clearance is seen with prematurity.

324
00:45:15,808 --> 00:45:20,871
With excessive serum bilirubin levels, toxicity has two forms.

325
00:45:20,871 --> 00:45:31,258
Acute bilirubin encephalopathy develops in the first days of life and may cause hypotonia, poor feeding, lethargy, and abnormal auditory-evoked responses.

326
00:45:31,258 --> 00:45:36,758
Immediate recognition and treatment will usually mitigate progressive neurotoxicity.

327
00:45:36,758 --> 00:45:39,796
The chronic form is termed kernicterus.

328
00:45:39,796 --> 00:45:47,858
With this, profound neuronal degeneration follows bilirubin deposition and staining of the basal ganglia and hippocampus.

329
00:45:47,858 --> 00:45:54,821
Survivors have spasticity, muscular incoordination, and varying degrees of mental deficiencies.

330
00:45:54,821 --> 00:46:03,083
Continuing hemolysis is a risk factor for kernicterus (El Houchi, 2017; Vandborg, 2012).

331
00:46:03,083 --> 00:46:08,971
Initial prevention emphasizes effective breastfeeding to avoid neonatal dehydration.

332
00:46:08,971 --> 00:46:15,371
Surveillance for jaundiced skin is recommended at the same time other newborn vital signs are obtained.

333
00:46:15,371 --> 00:46:26,446
Moreover, prior to hospital discharge, universal screening of total serum bilirubin assessment from blood or more commonly by transcutaneous bilirubinometry is done .

334
00:46:26,446 --> 00:46:33,833
The latter is a handheld meter that directs light into the skin and measures the intensity of specific returned wavelengths.

335
00:46:33,833 --> 00:46:40,521
Once diagnosed, various forms of phototherapy are used to treat neonatal hyperbilirubinemia.

336
00:46:40,521 --> 00:46:51,596
These “bili-lights” emit a spectrum of 460 to 490 nm, which augments bilirubin oxidation to enhance its renal clearance and lower serum levels.

337
00:46:51,596 --> 00:46:56,996
Sunlight filtered to remove ultraviolet light can be used in resource-poor countries .

338
00:46:56,996 --> 00:47:03,871
Light that penetrates the skin also raises peripheral blood flow, which further enhances photo-oxidation.

339
00:47:03,871 --> 00:47:18,196
For term newborns, the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) stress early detection and prompt phototherapy to prevent bilirubin encephalopathy.

340
00:47:18,196 --> 00:47:26,021
Despite these measures, bilirubin encephalopathy persists, and this is somewhat related to early hospital discharges .

341
00:47:26,021 --> 00:47:34,671
In the United States, hospitalizations for kernicterus in term newborns were 5 cases per 100,000 in 1988 .

342
00:47:34,671 --> 00:47:41,696
Since then, this rate has dropped to 0.4 to 2.7 cases per 100,000 births .

343
00:47:41,696 --> 00:47:47,208
Legislation to minimize brief postpartum hospital stays may be contributory .

344
00:47:47,208 --> 00:47:50,533
# Vitamin K Deficiency Bleeding.

345
00:47:50,533 --> 00:48:01,521
Newborns are vitamin K deficient due to insufficient antenatal liver stores, limited synthesis in an initially sterile gut, and low vitamin K content in breast milk.

346
00:48:01,521 --> 00:48:12,333
Vitamin K deficiency bleeding (VKDB) describes spontaneous internal or external bleeding, and its onset is grouped as early, classic, or late onset.

347
00:48:12,333 --> 00:48:18,683
Most cases result from abnormally low levels of the vitamin K– dependent clotting factors.

348
00:48:18,683 --> 00:48:25,521
Early onset is within the first 24 hours, and one cause is maternal vitamin K deficiency.

349
00:48:25,521 --> 00:48:34,346
Classic hemorrhagic disease is usually apparent between days 2 and 7 of life in neonates not given vitamin K prophylaxis at delivery.

350
00:48:34,346 --> 00:48:39,946
Delayed hemorrhage may occur at 2 to 12 weeks in exclusively breastfed infants.

351
00:48:39,946 --> 00:48:56,833
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend routine prophylaxis with a single, intramuscular, 0.5- to 1-mg dose of vitamin K1 (phytonadione).

352
00:48:56,833 --> 00:49:04,846
Oral administration is less effective, and maternal vitamin K administration results in very little transport to the fetus .

353
00:49:04,996 --> 00:49:13,846
Evidence does not support initial links with vitamin K and childhood leukemia (Fear, 2003; Roman, 2002).

354
00:49:13,796 --> 00:49:20,058
Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn includes VKDB but also bleeding from other etiologies.

355
00:49:20,058 --> 00:49:29,196
These include congenital clotting factor deficiency, disseminated intravascular coagulopathy, birth trauma, and thrombocytopenia.

356
00:49:29,196 --> 00:49:31,896
# Thrombocytopenia.

357
00:49:31,896 --> 00:49:42,383
Abnormally low platelet concentrations in term newborns are often categorized as early onset, within the first 72 hours, and late onset thereafter.

358
00:49:42,383 --> 00:49:57,371
In the term neonate, causes include immune disorders, infections, inherited platelet defects, or congenital syndromes (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019b; Cremer, 2016).

359
00:49:57,371 --> 00:50:10,058
Of immunologic sources, neonatal thrombocytopenia stemming from alloimmunization or from maternal idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura are described in Chapter 18 (p. 359).

360
00:50:10,058 --> 00:50:14,408
Neonatal lupus syndrome can also show thrombocytopenia .

361
00:50:14,408 --> 00:50:21,433
With all these, antiplatelet IgG transferred to the fetus may cause accelerated platelet destruction.

362
00:50:21,433 --> 00:50:34,596
Alternatively, thrombocytopenia can be an extension of fetal infection with B19 parvovirus, cytomegalovirus, toxoplasmosis, and others discussed in Chapters 67 and 68.

363
00:50:34,596 --> 00:50:44,221
Neonatal thrombocytopenia has been reported with maternal antiretroviral therapy for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection .

364
00:50:44,221 --> 00:50:48,796
Term newborns with sepsis can have accelerated platelet consumption .

365
00:50:48,796 --> 00:50:55,446
Last, inherited platelet disorders are described in Chapter 59 (p. 1059).

366
00:50:55,446 --> 00:50:57,533
Newborn Injuries.

367
00:50:57,533 --> 00:50:59,696
# Incidence.

368
00:50:59,696 --> 00:51:12,133
Birth injury can complicate any term delivery, and from population studies, its incidence approximates 2 percent of deliveries (Linder, 2012; SauberSchatz, 2010).

369
00:51:12,133 --> 00:51:18,508
Imprecision in these numbers may stem from lumping of major and minor injuries and trauma definitions.

370
00:51:18,508 --> 00:51:32,258
In one population study from Washington State, the rate of neonatal birth trauma declined by 14 percent and from 5.3 events per 1000 live births in 2004 to 4.5 in 2013 .

371
00:51:32,258 --> 00:51:40,421
Major injury rates were highest with forceps or vacuum delivery and substantially lower for all caesarean delivery categories.

372
00:51:40,421 --> 00:51:43,058
# Cranial Injuries.

373
00:51:43,058 --> 00:51:48,958
The fetal head has considerable plasticity and can undergo appreciable molding during labor.

374
00:51:48,958 --> 00:51:56,658
However, traumatic head injuries can occur and result in bony fracture or in intracranial or extracranial hemorrhage.

375
00:51:56,658 --> 00:52:03,033
With intra- and extracranial hemorrhages, anatomical location defines their classification .

376
00:52:03,033 --> 00:52:05,658
Table 33-4.

377
00:52:05,658 --> 00:52:14,471
Major Types of Cranial Hemorrhage Intracranial Hemorrhage Most aspects of neonatal intracranial hemorrhage are related to gestational age.

378
00:52:14,471 --> 00:52:23,383
Of the categories shown in Table 33-4, the term newborn may suffer subdural and intracerebral hemorrhages most often.

379
00:52:23,383 --> 00:52:30,358
Intraventricular, subarachnoid, or cerebellar hemorrhages are more frequent in the preterm neonate.

380
00:52:30,358 --> 00:52:40,796
In those born preterm, blood pressure instability, hypoxia, and ischemia are prominent causes and described in Chapter 34 (p. 618).

381
00:52:40,796 --> 00:52:45,383
In term neonates, birth trauma is the most frequent etiology.

382
00:52:45,383 --> 00:52:56,146
Other uncommon causes or contributors include acquired or congenital fetal coagulopathy, vascular anomaly, tumor, genetic mutation, and infection .

383
00:52:56,146 --> 00:53:04,871
Importantly, in some newborns, intracranial hemorrhage may follow an apparently uneventful vaginal delivery, and no cause is found.

384
00:53:04,871 --> 00:53:13,971
Term newborns with intracranial hemorrhage may initially display seizures, apnea or tachypnea, poor feeding, and temperature instability .

385
00:53:13,971 --> 00:53:19,758
Imaging options are sonography or CT, but MR imaging is often preferred.

386
00:53:19,758 --> 00:53:23,971
With most lesions, treatment is supportive and nonsurgical.

387
00:53:23,971 --> 00:53:32,758
However, acute hydrocephalus, rising intracranial pressure, or mass effects are infrequent complications that can prompt surgery .

388
00:53:32,758 --> 00:53:36,471
Names of intracranial lesions reflect anatomy.

389
00:53:36,471 --> 00:53:43,883
First, the meninges forms from the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater, and the dura mater is the external layer.

390
00:53:43,883 --> 00:53:48,408
Subdural hemorrhage collects between the dura and arachnoid mater.

391
00:53:48,408 --> 00:54:00,046
Prolonged and difficult delivery can produce cranial molding, dural stretch, and tearing of bridging veins in the subdural space (Towner, 1999; Hong, 2018).

392
00:54:00,046 --> 00:54:06,021
Asymptomatic subdural hemorrhage can also accompany uneventful vaginal or cesarean

393
00:54:06,172 --> 00:54:07,759
delivery .

394
00:54:07,709 --> 00:54:11,622
In asymptomatic newborns, prognosis is good .

395
00:54:11,622 --> 00:54:16,347
Poorer outcome may attend those with comorbid conditions or lesions .

396
00:54:16,347 --> 00:54:22,097
Less commonly, subarachnoid hemorrhage collects between the arachnoid and pia mater.

397
00:54:22,097 --> 00:54:28,072
Rarely, epidural hemorrhage collects outside the dura mater and beneath the periosteum.

398
00:54:28,072 --> 00:54:38,647
In both these hemorrhage types, risks, mechanisms, and recovery are similar to subdural hemorrhage (Hong, 2018; Shah, 2016).

399
00:54:38,647 --> 00:54:43,597
Intraventricular hemorrhage is less common in term than preterm newborns.

400
00:54:43,597 --> 00:54:53,734
Described in Chapter 34 (p. 618), the vascular germinal matrix is a vulnerable site for preterm neonates but has mainly regressed by term.

401
00:54:53,734 --> 00:55:00,647
Instead, the choroid plexus, thalamus, or residual germinal matrix are bleeding sources at term.

402
00:55:00,647 --> 00:55:06,459
Etiology can include birth trauma, hypoxia, or coagulation dysfunction.

403
00:55:06,459 --> 00:55:11,022
Additional rare causes are listed in this section’s first paragraph.

404
00:55:11,022 --> 00:55:20,259
However, in small case series, one to two thirds had no identified antecedent (Roland, 1990; Wu, 2003).

405
00:55:20,259 --> 00:55:27,309
Prognosis varies, and worse outcomes are associated with advanced hemorrhage grade or thalamic hemorrhage .

406
00:55:27,309 --> 00:55:32,659
Intracerebral hemorrhage or neonatal hemorrhagic stroke is uncommon at term.

407
00:55:32,659 --> 00:55:42,984
Risk factors are coagulation defects, vascular anomalies, and intrapartum factors that include FHR abnormalities and low Apgar scores.

408
00:55:42,984 --> 00:55:45,847
Trauma is less often implicated .

409
00:55:45,847 --> 00:55:49,984
Intracerebellar hemorrhage is infrequent in the term newborn.

410
00:55:49,984 --> 00:56:00,834
In one series, associated factors were FHR abnormalities, operative vaginal delivery, emergent cesarean delivery, and delivery-room resuscitation .

411
00:56:00,834 --> 00:56:13,647
Prognosis with both intracerebral and intracerebellar hemorrhages is affected by lesion size, presence of additional hemorrhage sites, and associated hydrocephalus or elevated intracranial pressure.

412
00:56:13,647 --> 00:56:18,959
Extracranial Hemorrhage These blood collections accumulate outside the calvarium .

413
00:56:18,959 --> 00:56:30,384
From its most superficial surface inward, the scalp consists of skin, subcutaneous tissue, galea aponeurotica, subgaleal space, and calvarium periosteum .

414
00:56:30,384 --> 00:56:38,434
The galea aponeurotica is dense fibrous tissue, whereas the subgaleal space contains loose, fibroareolar tissue.

415
00:56:38,434 --> 00:56:47,459
Traversing across the subgaleal space are emissary veins, which connect the dural sinuses inside the skull with superficial scalp veins.

416
00:56:47,459 --> 00:56:49,972
Figure 33-2.

417
00:56:49,972 --> 00:56:58,372
Schematic of extracranial lesions in the neonate that include caput succedaneum, subgaleal hemorrhage, and cephalohematoma.

418
00:56:58,372 --> 00:57:02,459
Caput succedaneum is soft-tissue edema of the scalp.

419
00:57:02,459 --> 00:57:08,084
It forms from repetitive contractions that press the head against an unyielding cervix.

420
00:57:08,084 --> 00:57:15,022
The caput is maximal at birth, rapidly grows smaller, and usually disappears within hours or days.

421
00:57:15,022 --> 00:57:19,522
Cephalohematoma is a cranial subperiosteal hematoma.

422
00:57:19,522 --> 00:57:28,422
It has an incidence of 1 percent and makes up a large percentage of birth injuries (Werner, 2011; Kekki, 2020).

423
00:57:28,422 --> 00:57:33,584
It usually develops in the setting of dystocia or operative vaginal delivery.

424
00:57:33,584 --> 00:57:43,972
Vacuum-assisted births have a greater association than births by forceps or cesarean delivery (Alexander, 2006; Werner, 2011).

425
00:57:43,972 --> 00:57:52,022
Cephalohematoma develops from shearing forces during labor and delivery, which lacerate the emissary or diploic veins.

426
00:57:52,022 --> 00:57:59,372
Fortunately, the densely adhered periosteum impedes rapid enlargement and limits final hematoma size.

427
00:57:59,372 --> 00:58:05,784
Hemorrhage can be over one or both parietal bones, but collections do not cross suture lines.

428
00:58:05,784 --> 00:58:13,509
A cephalohematoma may not be apparent until hours after delivery, when bleeding is sufficient to raise the periosteum.

429
00:58:13,509 --> 00:58:22,922
After it is identified, it often grows larger and persists for weeks or even months, and bleeding may cause anemia or hyperbilirubinemia.

430
00:58:22,922 --> 00:58:26,722
Outcomes are good in the absence of additional injuries.

431
00:58:26,722 --> 00:58:35,447
Rare hematoma complications are infection or calcification (Kandemirli, 2020; Zimmermann, 2016).

432
00:58:35,447 --> 00:58:44,384
Subgaleal hemorrhage is rare and results from emissary vein laceration and bleeding between the galea aponeurotica and the skull periosteum.

433
00:58:44,384 --> 00:58:51,872
Both the galea aponeurotica and subgaleal space span across the occipital, parietal, and frontal bones.

434
00:58:51,872 --> 00:59:00,684
Because of its loose areolar tissue and large surface area, significant blood volumes can collect in this potential space and may extend from

435
00:59:00,844 --> 00:59:05,731
the neck to the orbits and laterally to the temporal fascia above the ears .

436
00:59:05,681 --> 00:59:19,644
Resulting hypovolemia can lead to shock and significant morbidity, and cited mortality rates approximate 15 percent (El Dib, 2019; Lee, 2018; Swanson, 2011).

437
00:59:19,644 --> 00:59:25,394
Other findings are seizures, enlarging head circumference, and respiratory distress.

438
00:59:25,394 --> 00:59:34,019
The subgaleal hemorrhage rate is 0.05 percent with spontaneous birth but is ten times greater with vacuum-assisted delivery .

439
00:59:34,019 --> 00:59:44,256
The most frequent associate is operative vaginal delivery, especially with vacuum devices, but cases can follow spontaneous vaginal or cesarean delivery .

440
00:59:44,256 --> 00:59:49,781
In general, diagnosis of extracranial lesions begins with head ultrasound .

441
00:59:49,781 --> 00:59:53,269
This may be sufficient for cephalohematomas.

442
00:59:53,269 --> 01:00:00,506
CT or MR imaging may benefit those with subgaleal hemorrhage to exclude comorbid cranial injuries.

443
01:00:00,506 --> 01:00:07,381
Skull Fractures These are rare but are especially worrisome because of their association with intracranial hemorrhage.

444
01:00:07,381 --> 01:00:15,394
The incidence of birth-related skull fractures approximates 1 case per 100,000 births (Högberg, 2020).

445
01:00:15,394 --> 01:00:19,294
Operative vaginal delivery is an associated factor .

446
01:00:19,294 --> 01:00:28,469
However, cases can also attend spontaneous or cesarean delivery (Dolivet, 2018; Merhar, 2016).

447
01:00:28,469 --> 01:00:42,481
In these, fracture may result from tight skull compression against the bony pelvis, by hand pressure used by the surgeon to lift the head at cesarean delivery, or from transvaginally applied upward hand pressure by an assistant.

448
01:00:42,481 --> 01:00:51,356
Fractures are diagnosed with radiographs or CT. Depending on type, a fracture may be managed expectantly or require surgery.

449
01:00:51,356 --> 01:00:56,881
Prognosis is mainly influenced by comorbid intra- or extracranial injuries.

450
01:00:56,881 --> 01:00:59,456
Figure 33-3.

451
01:00:59,456 --> 01:01:04,219
Depressed skull fracture evident immediately after cesarean delivery.

452
01:01:04,219 --> 01:01:08,244
Labor had progressed, and the head was deep in the pelvis.

453
01:01:08,244 --> 01:01:15,269
Dislodgment of the head from the birth canal was performed by an assistant using manual pressure upward through the vagina.

454
01:01:15,269 --> 01:01:21,506
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Kimberly M. Spoonts.) # Spinal Cord Injury.

455
01:01:21,506 --> 01:01:26,781
Overstretch of the spinal cord and its associated hemorrhage and edema are rare.

456
01:01:26,781 --> 01:01:44,269
The cervical spine is most often injured, and excessive traction, rotation, or hyperextension during shoulder dystocia manipulations, breech vaginal delivery, and operative vaginal delivery are implicated (Lee, 2020a; Menticoglou, 1995).

457
01:01:44,269 --> 01:01:50,469
Upper cervical spinal cord injury is often fatal, and survivors can be quadriplegic .

458
01:01:50,469 --> 01:01:57,494
Immediate treatment has included ventilator support coupled with corticosteroids or therapeutic hypothermia.

459
01:01:57,494 --> 01:02:00,444
# Peripheral Nerve Injuries.

460
01:02:00,444 --> 01:02:07,269
Brachial Plexopathy Injury can involve a single nerve or can affect a nerve root, plexus, or trunk.

461
01:02:07,269 --> 01:02:10,544
Trauma to the brachial plexus is infrequent.

462
01:02:10,544 --> 01:02:16,956
In one database study, the rate was 1 case per 1000 term births in the United States.

463
01:02:16,956 --> 01:02:24,906
The incidence was 1.3 per 1000 vaginal deliveries and 0.2 per 1000 cesarean deliveries .

464
01:02:24,906 --> 01:02:29,969
Shoulder dystocia, labor dystocia, and breech delivery are risks .

465
01:02:29,969 --> 01:02:37,481
With plexopathy, the injury damages the nerve roots that supply the brachial plexus—C5–8 and T1.

466
01:02:37,481 --> 01:02:44,456
With hemorrhage and edema, axonal function may be temporarily impaired, but the recovery chances are good.

467
01:02:44,456 --> 01:02:49,444
However, with avulsion, the prognosis for complete recovery is poor.

468
01:02:49,444 --> 01:02:53,956
Damage to the C5–6 nerve roots can cause Erb palsy.

469
01:02:53,956 --> 01:03:02,869
Injuries with breech delivery are normally of this type, whereas the more extensive and persistent lesions can follow difficult cephalic deliveries.

470
01:03:02,869 --> 01:03:07,944
The latter are more often forceps or vacuum-assisted vaginal deliveries .

471
01:03:07,944 --> 01:03:18,881
The C5–6 roots join to form the upper trunk of the plexus, and injury leads to paralysis of the deltoid, infraspinatus, and flexor muscles of the forearm.

472
01:03:18,881 --> 01:03:26,169
The affected arm is held straight and internally rotated, the elbow is extended, and the wrist and fingers flexed.

473
01:03:26,169 --> 01:03:29,194
Finger function usually is retained.

474
01:03:29,194 --> 01:03:36,969
Damage to the C8-T1 roots supplying the lower plexus results in Klumpke palsy, in which the hand is flaccid.

475
01:03:36,969 --> 01:03:43,556
With total involvement of all brachial plexus nerve roots, flaccidity of the arm and hand results.

476
01:03:43,556 --> 01:03:52,269
Rarely, with severe injury, phrenic nerve damage can cause ipsilateral diaphragm paralysis, or injury to sympathetic nerve

477
01:03:52,420 --> 01:03:59,082
fibers can cause Horner syndrome, which manifests as ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis .

478
01:03:59,032 --> 01:04:05,695
Clavicle or humeral fracture can be comorbid, and clinical concern may prompt chest radiography.

479
01:04:05,695 --> 01:04:09,945
MR imaging may be obtained with concern for nerve avulsion .

480
01:04:09,945 --> 01:04:21,470
Because of the importance of brachial plexopathy, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2014b) convened a task force to review extant studies.

481
01:04:21,470 --> 01:04:31,507
This Task Force concluded that shoulder dystocia cannot be accurately predicted, but in most cases, axonal death does not occur and the prognosis is good.

482
01:04:31,507 --> 01:04:45,582
Lindqvist and associates (2012) reported complete recovery in 86 percent of children with C5–6 trauma, which was the most common injury, and in 38 percent of those with C5–7 damage.

483
01:04:45,582 --> 01:04:52,070
However, those with global C5–8–T1 injuries always had permanent disability.

484
01:04:52,070 --> 01:04:57,495
Early physical therapy is initiated to prevent contractures or joint deformity.

485
01:04:57,495 --> 01:05:06,570
Maintaining passive joint motion, promoting extremity use, strengthening affected muscles, and providing compensatory techniques are goals .

486
01:05:06,570 --> 01:05:18,082
Severe injuries may require surgical exploration and nerve reconstruction, usually at 3 to 6 months (Vuillermin, 2016; Wilson, 2016).

487
01:05:18,082 --> 01:05:26,632
Facial Paralysis The facial nerve can be injured as it emerges from the stylomastoid foramen, and this may cause facial paralysis .

488
01:05:26,632 --> 01:05:32,595
From one series, the incidence was 0.3 cases per 1000 term births .

489
01:05:32,595 --> 01:05:39,407
Of these vaginal births, forceps were used in one fourth, and three fourths followed spontaneous birth.

490
01:05:39,407 --> 01:05:46,032
Pressure from the forceps blade or the bony pelvis, respectively, are suggested injury mechanisms.

491
01:05:46,032 --> 01:05:48,782
It can follow cesarean delivery .

492
01:05:48,782 --> 01:05:55,757
Spontaneous recovery within a few days is the rule, however, permanent paralysis has been described .

493
01:05:55,757 --> 01:05:58,432
Figure 33-4.

494
01:05:58,432 --> 01:06:00,870
Left facial nerve injury.

495
01:06:00,870 --> 01:06:09,270
Classically, the eye fails to close tightly, the labionasal fold is lost, and the mouth pulls to the unaffected side during crying.

496
01:06:09,270 --> 01:06:13,445
This was almost completely resolved two days after delivery.

497
01:06:13,445 --> 01:06:15,682
# Fractures.

498
01:06:15,682 --> 01:06:21,820
Most long-bone fractures follow difficult deliveries, however, this is not always the case.

499
01:06:21,820 --> 01:06:29,207
At minimum, palpation of the clavicles and long bones is indicated for all newborns after a difficult delivery.

500
01:06:29,207 --> 01:06:33,970
Overlying crepitation or irregularity will prompt radiography.

501
01:06:33,970 --> 01:06:44,107
Clavicular fractures are common, unpredictable, and una-voidable complications of normal birth (Ahn, 2015; Roberts, 1995).

502
01:06:44,107 --> 01:06:52,307
Their incidence approximates 5 cases in 1000 live births (Kekki, 2020; Linder, 2012).

503
01:06:52,307 --> 01:06:57,120
Rates are tenfold higher with vaginal birth than with cesarean delivery .

504
01:06:57,120 --> 01:07:03,095
With vaginal birth, shoulder dystocia is an association (Högberg, 2020).

505
01:07:03,095 --> 01:07:07,232
With cesarean delivery, greater birth weight is a factor .

506
01:07:07,232 --> 01:07:10,820
Humeral fractures and femoral fractures are rare.

507
01:07:10,820 --> 01:07:22,395
For the humerus, associated settings are breech delivery or posterior arm delivery during shoulder dystocia release (Rietberg, 2003; von Heideken, 2020).

508
01:07:22,395 --> 01:07:26,932
Those of the femur are usually associated with breech presentation.

509
01:07:26,932 --> 01:07:34,795
Because most breech-presenting fetuses now undergo cesarean delivery, most of these fractures are associated with this mode .

510
01:07:34,795 --> 01:07:44,882
As potential prevention for both fracture types, careful splinting of the bone along its long axis and avoiding perpendicular forces during extraction are encouraged.

511
01:07:44,882 --> 01:07:48,220
Last, rib fractures are rare at term.

512
01:07:48,220 --> 01:07:55,232
In one series, none was associated with cardiopulmonary resuscitation (Högberg, 2020).

513
01:07:55,232 --> 01:08:03,882
Instead, compression forces to the chest during difficult birth or shoulder dystocia are implicated (van Rijn, 2009).

514
01:08:03,882 --> 01:08:06,732
# Soft Tissue Injury.

515
01:08:06,732 --> 01:08:13,145
Conceivably, any fetal organ or part could be injured with either vaginal or cesarean delivery.

516
01:08:13,145 --> 01:08:22,320
Intraabdominal hemorrhage is rare and most often stems from the liver, spleen, or adrenal glands, which are disproportionally large in the newborn.

517
01:08:22,320 --> 01:08:32,482
Risks are difficult delivery, especially those presenting breech; cardiac resuscitation; macrosomia; or pathologic enlargement of the involved organ .

518
01:08:32,482 --> 01:08:45,695
Retroperitoneal bleeding with these may manifest as bruising of the flanks, umbilicus, groin, upper thigh, or scrotum (Akin 2011; Heyman, 2011; Raveenthiran, 2008).

519
01:08:45,695 --> 01:08:50,007
Pale skin, distended abdomen, and falling hematocrit

520
01:08:50,164 --> 01:08:53,164
reflect associated hypovolemia.

521
01:08:53,114 --> 01:08:57,889
Treatment involves hemodynamic resuscitation and control of bleeding.

522
01:08:57,889 --> 01:09:01,139
# Congenital Deformity Injuries.

523
01:09:01,139 --> 01:09:07,789
In congenital torticollis, one sternocleidomastoid muscle fails to elongate sufficiently.

524
01:09:07,789 --> 01:09:11,489
The head gradually turns toward the side of the injury.

525
01:09:11,489 --> 01:09:14,126
Its etiology is unclear.

526
01:09:14,126 --> 01:09:20,776
Some cases are associated with breech presentation, operative vaginal birth, or difficult delivery.

527
01:09:20,776 --> 01:09:33,389
However, many follow uncomplicated birth, and intrauterine positioning, genetics, and ischemia are some suggested theories (Hardgrib, 2017; Sargent, 2019).

528
01:09:33,389 --> 01:09:36,376
Early physical therapy is recommended .

529
01:09:36,376 --> 01:09:45,314
With congenital deformations, a normally developed fetal structure becomes deformed usually by restrictive intrauterine mechanical forces.

530
01:09:45,314 --> 01:09:53,414
Common factors are chronic oligohydramnios, multifetal gestation, or abnormally shaped or small uterine cavity.

531
01:09:53,414 --> 01:10:06,389
Some mechanical deformations include talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) and oligohydramnios sequence (Hall, 2014; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2019).

532
01:10:06,389 --> 01:10:13,501
The latter may lead to pulmonary hypoplasia and deformations of the head, face, skin, and limbs.

533
01:10:13,501 --> 01:10:25,076
This sequence and talipes can also be associated with genetic abnormalities or malformations and are discussed in Chapters 14 and 15 (p. 260 and 303).

534
01:10:25,076 --> 01:10:31,726
Last, congenital hip dislocation is strongly associated with an in-utero breech presentation .

535
01:10:31,726 --> 01:10:36,189
Physical examination screening for all newborns is recommended.

536
01:10:36,189 --> 01:10:39,664
Treatment may include observation or bracing .

